{
  "title": "Java If-Else: Making Decisions",
  "dayLabel": "Day 11 / 100",
  "scenes": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "narration": "In real life, we make decisions every second. If the traffic light is green, you go. If it is red, you stop. Today, we teach your Java code how to think.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "TitleCard",
        "props": {
          "title": "Making Choices with If-Else",
          "subtitle": "Control flow for beginners",
          "badge": "DAY 11 • JAVA",
          "tags": [
            "Java",
            "Logic",
            "Beginner"
          ]
        },
        "chapter": "Control Flow"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "narration": "Think of a decision as a fork in the road. Your code reaches a point where it must choose one path or the other. We call this a condition.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "ArchitectureFlow",
        "props": {
          "title": "The Fork in the Road",
          "activeStepIndex": 0,
          "steps": [
            {
              "title": "Condition",
              "desc": "A question that results in True or False."
            },
            {
              "title": "True Path",
              "desc": "Code that runs if the answer is Yes."
            },
            {
              "title": "False Path",
              "desc": "Code that runs if the answer is No."
            }
          ]
        },
        "keyIdea": "Decisions create paths in code",
        "camera": [
          {
            "at": "fork",
            "zoom": 1.0,
            "x": 50,
            "y": 50
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "narration": "Why do we need this? Imagine a banking app. It should only let you withdraw money if your balance is high enough. Without if-else, it would just give money to everyone.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "SummaryList",
        "props": {
          "title": "Why Logic Matters",
          "items": [
            "Validate user input",
            "Secure sensitive actions",
            "Personalize the experience",
            "Handle errors gracefully"
          ]
        },
        "popups": [
          {
            "anchor": "withdraw",
            "text": "Logic protects the data",
            "kind": "note"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "narration": "Let's look at the map of an if-else statement. First comes the condition. If it is true, we enter the first block. If not, we skip to the else block.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "DiagramFlow",
        "props": {
          "title": "If-Else Logic Flow",
          "nodes": [
            {
              "id": "start",
              "label": "Start"
            },
            {
              "id": "cond",
              "label": "Is Age >= 18?",
              "sub": "Condition"
            },
            {
              "id": "ifBlock",
              "label": "Print 'Adult'"
            },
            {
              "id": "elseBlock",
              "label": "Print 'Minor'"
            }
          ],
          "edges": [
            {
              "from": "start",
              "to": "cond"
            },
            {
              "from": "cond",
              "to": "ifBlock",
              "label": "True"
            },
            {
              "from": "cond",
              "to": "elseBlock",
              "label": "False"
            }
          ],
          "steps": [
            {
              "at": "First",
              "label": "Check the condition",
              "focus": [
                "cond"
              ]
            },
            {
              "at": "true",
              "label": "Follow the true path",
              "arrow": 1
            },
            {
              "at": "else",
              "label": "Otherwise, follow false",
              "arrow": 2
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "narration": "Now, the smallest real code. We create an integer variable for age. Then we use the if keyword. This is the simplest way to start.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "TypingCode",
        "type_anchor": "We",
        "props": {
          "filename": "Main.java",
          "language": "java",
          "code": "int age = 20;\n\nif (age >= 18) {\n    System.out.println(\"You can vote!\");\n}",
          "highlightLines": [
            3
          ]
        },
        "camera": [
          {
            "at": "if",
            "zoom": 1.6,
            "x": 50,
            "y": 40
          }
        ],
        "popups": [
          {
            "anchor": "if",
            "text": "if is a keyword",
            "kind": "tip"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "narration": "Look at the parentheses. Inside, we put our condition. A condition is a comparison that results in a boolean, which means it is either true or false.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "TypingCode",
        "props": {
          "filename": "Main.java",
          "language": "java",
          "code": "int age = 20;\n\nif (age >= 18) {\n    System.out.println(\"You can vote!\");\n}",
          "highlightLines": [
            3
          ]
        },
        "camera": [
          {
            "at": "parentheses",
            "zoom": 1.8,
            "x": 40,
            "y": 35
          }
        ],
        "keyIdea": "Conditions are True or False"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 7,
      "narration": "The curly braces create a block. A block is a group of code that belongs together. Everything inside these braces only runs if the condition is true.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "TypingCode",
        "props": {
          "filename": "Main.java",
          "language": "java",
          "code": "int age = 20;\n\nif (age >= 18) {\n    System.out.println(\"You can vote!\");\n}",
          "highlightLines": [
            3,
            4,
            5
          ]
        },
        "camera": [
          {
            "at": "braces",
            "zoom": 1.5,
            "x": 50,
            "y": 50
          }
        ],
        "popups": [
          {
            "anchor": "block",
            "text": "Braces group your code",
            "kind": "note"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 8,
      "narration": "Let's run it. Since 20 is indeed greater than or equal to 18, the computer prints our message. If age was 15, nothing would happen.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "TerminalRun",
        "props": {
          "command": "java Main.java",
          "output": [
            "You can vote!"
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 9,
      "narration": "But we want to say something if the person is too young. We add the else block. Think of else as the 'otherwise' option.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "TypingCode",
        "type_anchor": "We",
        "props": {
          "filename": "Main.java",
          "language": "java",
          "code": "int age = 15;\n\nif (age >= 18) {\n    System.out.println(\"You can vote!\");\n} else {\n    System.out.println(\"Too young.\");\n}",
          "highlightLines": [
            5,
            6,
            7
          ]
        },
        "previous_code": "int age = 20;\n\nif (age >= 18) {\n    System.out.println(\"You can vote!\");\n}",
        "camera": [
          {
            "at": "else",
            "zoom": 1.6,
            "x": 50,
            "y": 65
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 10,
      "narration": "Now watch the output again. Since age is now 15, the first condition fails. The computer jumps straight to the else block and prints our second message.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "TerminalRun",
        "props": {
          "command": "java Main.java",
          "output": [
            "Too young."
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 11,
      "narration": "A common mistake beginners make is putting a semicolon right after the if statement. This tells Java the decision is over immediately, which breaks your logic.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "ComparisonCard",
        "props": {
          "title": "Avoid this Mistake",
          "leftTitle": "Wrong",
          "leftPoints": [
            "if (age > 18);",
            "Semicolon ends the if",
            "Code always runs"
          ],
          "rightTitle": "Correct",
          "rightPoints": [
            "if (age > 18) {",
            "Uses curly braces",
            "Logic works correctly"
          ]
        },
        "popups": [
          {
            "anchor": "semicolon",
            "text": "No semicolon after if()",
            "kind": "tip"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 12,
      "narration": "Let's recap. If checks a condition. Else provides a backup plan. Always use curly braces to keep your code organized and easy to read.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "SummaryList",
        "props": {
          "title": "Key Takeaways",
          "items": [
            "If starts the decision",
            "Conditions go in parentheses",
            "Else runs if the condition is false",
            "Braces define the block of code"
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    {
